            <a href="/best-sole-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole roundup</a>
            sets out those conflicts.
          </p>
          <p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall and warning databases for
            each brand. None of the six machines here is under an open recall or warning. Horizon's
            2022 recall covered the T101-05 folding treadmill, not the 7-series machines listed here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-cushioning-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. What a Cushioned Treadmill Actually Does</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill deck is a rigid board. What makes it feel cushioned is the way it is mounted:
            on elastomer isolators, springs or shaped mounts that let it move a fraction of an inch
            under each footstrike and spring back. That small movement spreads the moment of impact
            over slightly more time, which is why landing on a good treadmill feels softer than
            landing on a road.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it does well.</strong> It makes a session more comfortable, and comfort is not
            trivial: a surface that feels good is one you are more likely to use for the time you
            intended. It also reduces the sharpness of each landing compared with concrete or
            asphalt, which matters most to runners because running loads the legs far more per step
            than walking.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it does not do.</strong> It does not turn running into walking. The force
            through your legs is still set mostly by your body weight, speed and stride, and a deck
            that moves a fraction of an inch can only soften part of it. It does not make up for worn
            shoes, a sudden jump in training or an existing injury. And the shoes on your feet carry
            far more cushioning than any deck does.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Why softer is not always better.</strong> A deck that compresses a lot and returns
            slowly feels plush for a few minutes and then tiring, because some of each stride goes
            into the deck rather than into moving you forward. Independent reviewers describing the
            decks here use words like "middle", "firm" and "softer side" — and for most people, a
            deck in the middle is the one that still feels good at minute forty. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-cushioning-explained/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cushioning explainer</a>
            goes through deck suspension, belts, wear and how to judge a deck in a store.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="systems-compared" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The Cushioning Systems, Compared</h2>
<p>
            Brand names make the systems sound more different than they are. Underneath, they fall
            into three types, and knowing which one a machine uses tells you more than its name.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Zoned systems (Horizon, Bowflex).</strong> Horizon's 3-Zone Variable Response
            Cushioning and Bowflex's FlexZone both vary the support along the deck. Bowflex is
            explicit: softer at the front where the foot lands, supported in the middle, firmer at
            the rear for push-off. The idea is a soft landing without a sluggish push-off, and it is
            the most common design on good mid-priced treadmills. It suits mixed walking and running
            well, as long as you land on the cushioned zone rather than right at the front edge —
            another reason a 60-inch deck is worth having.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Gym-derived systems (Life Fitness).</strong> FlexDeck is the shock absorption
            system Life Fitness names on several of its treadmills, built around shock absorbers that
            its warranty terms call Lifesprings. Its distinguishing feature here is the warranty: a retailer and an
            independent review both list lifetime cover on those shock absorbers, which acknowledges
            that cushioning parts are what wear.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Uniform cushioned decks (NordicTrack, ProForm).</strong> NordicTrack's RunFlex and
            ProForm's ProShox are described by their makers as cushioned decks that reduce joint impact,
            without a published zone layout. Some NordicTrack retailers describe a selectable
            soft-or-firm setting on the Commercial 1750; NordicTrack's own product text does not, so
            treat that as something to confirm.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Adjustable systems.</strong> A few machines let you change firmness with a lever or
            dial, which is useful in a household where one person walks and another runs. None of the
            six here is clearly documented as adjustable by its manufacturer, which is worth knowing
            if you have seen the claim elsewhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="impact-claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Shock Absorbing Treadmill Claims: What the Percentages Mean</h2>
<p>
            If you are shopping for a shock absorbing treadmill, you will see figures such as "reduces
            impact by up to 30%" or "27% more cushioning than the nearest competitor". NordicTrack's X16
            page makes the second claim, for example, and Sole's own blog says its deck reduces joint
            impact by up to 40%.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of these are false, necessarily, but none can be compared. There is no widely adopted
            independent standard for measuring treadmill shock absorption. Each maker measures against
            a baseline it chooses — often road or concrete, sometimes its own previous deck — using a
            method it rarely publishes. A 40% figure from one brand and a 30% figure from another tell
            you nothing about which deck is softer, firmer or better suited to you.
          </p>
          <p>
            Better signals are easier to read. A <strong>named system with a described design</strong>
            (zones, shock absorbers) tells you something was engineered. A <strong>warranty that covers
            the cushioning parts</strong> tells you the maker expects them to last. <strong>Several
            independent descriptions of how the deck feels</strong> tell you whether it leans soft or
            firm. And if you can stand on one in a store, a few minutes at your own pace in your own
            shoes beats any percentage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="low-impact" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Making Any Treadmill a Low Impact Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The deck is one part of a low impact treadmill. How you use it is the larger part, and
            these settings cost nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walk on an incline instead of running.</strong> Raising the grade at walking pace
            increases effort substantially without the higher landing forces of running. Every
            machine here reaches at least 12%, and the Horizon, Bowflex and Life Fitness machines reach
            15%. Our guide to
            <a href="/what-incline-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what incline to walk on</a>
            sets out a gradual progression.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use small speed steps and a gentle start.</strong> Many people find their
            comfortable pace sits in a narrow band, so change speed in small increments and build
            duration before speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Leave decline alone if your knees are sore.</strong> Downhill walking loads the
            front of the knee more than flat or uphill work. The NordicTrack 1750 offers decline; it is
            a feature to use deliberately, not by default.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check your shoes and your floor.</strong> Worn shoes undo a good deck, and a
            treadmill on thick carpet can shift slightly under each step. A firm, level base with a
            proper mat helps stability.
          </p>
          <p>
            If joint pain is the reason you are shopping, our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for bad knees</a>
            guide focuses on incline, speed control and building up, rather than cushioning, and
            that is the right emphasis. This is general information, not medical advice; a doctor or
            physical therapist who knows your history is the right person to ask what kind of exercise
            suits you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Choosing Between Them</h2>
<p>
            The six machines split by how you train and what you are prepared to pay for, more than by
            how soft they are.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Mostly walking, some jogging.</strong> The Horizon 7.0 AT gives you a named zoned
            system, 15% incline and a lifetime motor for about $1,000, and an independent review
            describes its deck as on the softer side, which most walkers like. Spend more on the 7.8
            AT only if you want the wider 22-inch deck or the higher weight limit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Regular running.</strong> A firm, responsive deck is usually less tiring over a long
            run than a soft one. The Horizon 7.8 AT and the Bowflex T9 both use zoned systems with a
            firmer rear section for push-off, both have 22-inch decks, and both carry lifetime motor
            cover. The T9 is cheaper; the 7.8 AT has the higher weight limit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Classes and screens.</strong> If you will follow trainer-led iFIT workouts, the
            NordicTrack Commercial 1750 adds decline and a 16-inch screen to its RunFlex deck, and the
            ProForm Carbon Pro 2000 does the same job for less with a shorter motor warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Longevity above all.</strong> The Life Fitness Run CX costs far more, does not fold
            and has a firm deck, but it is the only machine here with lifetime cover listed on its
            shock absorbers, a 10-year motor warranty and a 6-inch step-up. For a heavy daily user who
            wants to buy once, that is what the extra money buys.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. What About Cushioned Walking Pads?</h2>
<p>
            Walking pad listings often promise multi-layer shock absorption, and some do feel softer
            than a bare floor. But a walking pad is a thin, light machine with a short, narrow belt
            and little room under the deck for suspension. At walking pace that is usually fine, since
            walking loads the legs much less than running. It is not a substitute for a full-size
            cushioned deck if you intend to run, or if you are heavier and want the deck to stay
            stable.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want a walking pad anyway, choose on belt length, weight capacity and how it fits
            your space rather than on a cushioning claim; our
            <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for heavy people</a>
            roundup covers the sturdier ones.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If impact is the problem rather than the surface.</strong> If even walking on level
            ground is painful, a softer treadmill will not change the basic loading. A recumbent
            bike or an elliptical removes the impact phase entirely; our
            <a href="/treadmill-vs-elliptical/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs elliptical</a>
            comparison weighs the options.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need a quiet machine for downstairs neighbours.</strong> More cushioning is
            not the same as less noise below. Frame mass, a dense mat and where the machine sits matter
            as much; our <a href="/quiet-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quiet treadmills</a>
            guide explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need it to fit a small room.</strong> Every machine here has a 60-inch deck
            and a large footprint, and the Life Fitness Run CX does not fold. Our
            <a href="/best-low-profile-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">low-profile treadmills</a>
            roundup covers slimmer machines.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best cushioned treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, the Horizon 7.8 AT: Horizon documents its 3-Zone Variable Response Cushioning, it has a 22 by 60-inch deck, 15% incline, a 375 lb capacity and lifetime frame and motor cover, and it needs no subscription. The Bowflex T9 is a cheaper zoned alternative, and the Life Fitness Run CX is the premium choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cushioned treadmill better for your joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is more comfortable than pavement, and many people with sore joints prefer it. But speed, incline, shoes and how gradually you build up usually affect how your joints feel more than the deck does. This is general information, not medical advice; ask a doctor or physical therapist about a specific condition.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What makes a treadmill low impact?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly how you use it. Walking on an incline raises effort without the higher landing forces of running, and a cushioned deck softens each landing compared with a road. A full-size deck on a stiff frame, a gradual build-up, good shoes and leaving decline alone if your knees are sore all help.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are treadmill impact-reduction percentages accurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They cannot be checked or compared. There is no widely adopted independent standard for treadmill shock absorption, so each manufacturer measures against its own baseline using its own method. Treat percentages as marketing, and look instead for a described system, a warranty on the cushioning parts and independent descriptions of the deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a softer treadmill deck always better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A deck that compresses a lot and returns slowly can feel tiring over a long session, because some of each stride goes into the deck. Most people are better served by moderate give with a quick return. Independent reviews describe the decks on this page as ranging from firm to the softer side.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do walking pads have good cushioning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some feel softer than a bare floor, but a walking pad has little room for suspension and a short, narrow belt. That is usually fine at walking pace. If you want to run, or want a stable, cushioned deck for heavier use, a full-size treadmill with a named cushioning system is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

